The chemistry points first to Ca–W–O products plus residual WO₃/CaCO₃ or decomposition products, but I’ll let the peak families decide. I’m now extracting robust peak positions and querying only the frozen COD index for exact and chemically plausible phases.

Peak-family matching is now internally consistent with two crystalline phases: tetragonal scheelite CaWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃. The sharp WO₃ triplet near 22.8–24.0° and its split higher-angle families distinguish it from tetragonal/hexagonal WO₃ references; the broad scheelite family accounts for the dominant peaks. I’m building the first full-grid two-phase GSAS-II checkpoint now, before testing alternative WO₃ topologies or any weak third phase.

The first complete checkpoint fits the full grid at Rwp 4.28% (statistical minimum 3.14%) with about 85.45 wt% CaWO₄ and 14.55 wt% WO₃. Residuals are mostly positive/negative lobes at already assigned reflections rather than a new coherent peak family. I’m now running the required same-state topology checks for WO₃; these use identical scale, cell, calibration, and profile stages so a lower-symmetry model is not favored merely by extra relaxation.

